window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["crewai"]=[{"id":"crewai-f0001","card":"q-crewai-001-1-core-concepts","sec":"crewai-s002","q":"What is CrewAI, and what problem does it solve compared with a single LLM call?","a":"<p>CrewAI is a Python framework for multi-agent systems. A team of LLM-powered agents, each with a role and tools, works through tasks and hands results to the next agent. A single prompt has to do everything at once. Splitting the work into focused agents and tasks gives each step a clear objective and makes the output easier to inspect and improve.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0002","card":"q-crewai-001-1-core-concepts","sec":"crewai-s002","q":"Name the four core building blocks of CrewAI and the job of each.","a":"<p><strong>Agent</strong>: an LLM-powered worker with a role, goal and optional tools. <strong>Task</strong>: one actionable step with a description and expected output. <strong>Crew</strong>: the orchestrator that holds agents and tasks, runs them and collects the result. <strong>Process</strong>: how tasks are ordered and assigned (<code>sequential</code> or <code>hierarchical</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0003","card":"q-crewai-001-1-core-concepts","sec":"crewai-s002","q":"In CrewAI, which building block is autonomous, and what does that mean for the rest?","a":"<p>The agent is the autonomous part: it decides how to reach its task's goal, can use tools and can delegate. Tasks, crews and processes are structure around it. The crew only orchestrates, so it behaves like a project manager rather than a decision maker (except in hierarchical mode, where a manager agent decides).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0004","card":"q-crewai-001-1-core-concepts","sec":"crewai-s002","q":"In CrewAI, what are Tools, Memory and Knowledge, and how do they differ?","a":"<p>They are supplementary components. Tools let agents take external actions (search, read files, call APIs). Memory keeps information from earlier interactions across tasks or runs. Knowledge is domain-specific reference data (files, documents) the agent can retrieve from. Tools act, memory remembers what happened, knowledge supplies facts you provide.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0005","card":"q-crewai-001-1-core-concepts","sec":"crewai-s002","q":"What are the two factors that most decide whether a CrewAI project works well?","a":"<p>Accurately defined agents (clear role, goal, backstory) and well-described tasks (clear description and expected output). Vague tasks give vague results regardless of the model, so task breakdown is where most design effort should go.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0006","card":"q-crewai-001-1-core-concepts","sec":"crewai-s002","q":"When is a multi-agent crew a poor choice compared with a single agent or plain prompt?","a":"<p>When the job is one simple step, needs low latency, or must be cheap. Every extra agent adds LLM calls, tokens, latency and more places to go wrong. Use a crew when the work splits into distinct steps that benefit from different roles, tools or models, such as research, then outline, then write.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0007","card":"q-crewai-002-2-setup-and-project-types","sec":"crewai-s003","q":"Which Python versions does CrewAI support, and why does that matter for setup? (Version-sensitive: stated for the CrewAI 0.x/1.x releases around 2025.)","a":"<p>CrewAI required Python 3.10 or newer and older than 3.14 in the 2025 releases (3.10 to 3.13). Check the current docs, because the range moves with releases. If your system Python is outside the range, use a tool such as <code>uv</code> that can fetch a supported interpreter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0008","card":"q-crewai-002-2-setup-and-project-types","sec":"crewai-s003","q":"Why does the CrewAI CLI recommend installing with <code>uv tool install crewai</code> instead of <code>pip install crewai</code>?","a":"<p><code>uv tool install</code> puts the <code>crewai</code> CLI in its own isolated environment on your PATH, so it does not clash with project dependencies. <code>uv</code> is also much faster than pip at resolving and installing. The CLI is the tool that scaffolds and runs projects, while your project's own libraries are installed per project.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0009","card":"q-crewai-002-2-setup-and-project-types","sec":"crewai-s003","q":"In CrewAI, what do <code>crewai create crew &lt;name&gt;</code> and <code>crewai install</code> each do?","a":"<p><code>crewai create crew &lt;name&gt;</code> scaffolds a project: folders, <code>agents.yaml</code>, <code>tasks.yaml</code>, <code>crew.py</code>, <code>main.py</code>, <code>.env</code> and <code>pyproject.toml</code>. It prompts for provider, model and API key. <code>crewai install</code>, run at the project root, creates a virtual environment and installs the project's dependencies. Create first, install second.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0010","card":"q-crewai-002-2-setup-and-project-types","sec":"crewai-s003","q":"In a scaffolded CrewAI project, what goes in <code>config/agents.yaml</code>, <code>config/tasks.yaml</code>, <code>crew.py</code> and <code>main.py</code>?","a":"<p><code>agents.yaml</code> and <code>tasks.yaml</code> hold the declarative definitions (role, goal, backstory, description, expected output). <code>crew.py</code> is the Python class that turns those into <code>Agent</code>, <code>Task</code> and <code>Crew</code> objects with decorators and adds runtime settings such as tools. <code>main.py</code> is the entry point with <code>run</code>, <code>train</code>, <code>replay</code> and <code>test</code> functions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0011","card":"q-crewai-002-2-setup-and-project-types","sec":"crewai-s003","q":"In a scaffolded CrewAI project, what is the <code>knowledge/</code> folder for?","a":"<p>It holds files such as CSV, PDF or Excel that agents can use as private reference data (retrieval over your own documents). Use it for information the LLM would not know, rather than pasting it into prompts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0012","card":"q-crewai-002-2-setup-and-project-types","sec":"crewai-s003","q":"In a scaffolded CrewAI project, where do the model name and the provider API key go, and why there?","a":"<p>In the <code>.env</code> file, using the provider's standard variable name (for example <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code>) plus a <code>MODEL</code> entry. Keeping keys in <code>.env</code> keeps secrets out of source code. Add <code>.env</code> to <code>.gitignore</code> so it is never committed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0013","card":"q-crewai-002-2-setup-and-project-types","sec":"crewai-s003","q":"What does it usually mean when <code>crewai install</code> fails with a <code>psycopg2-binary</code> error, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The build needs PostgreSQL client libraries that are missing from the system, or their path is not global. Install PostgreSQL (for example <code>brew install postgresql</code> on macOS or <code>apt-get install postgresql postgresql-contrib</code> on Ubuntu) and run the install again.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0014","card":"q-crewai-003-from-scratch-vs-scaffolded","sec":"crewai-s004","q":"How do a from-scratch CrewAI project and a scaffolded one differ, and which would you use for production?","a":"<p>From scratch means a folder, a venv (<code>python -m venv .venv</code>) and <code>pip install crewai</code>, with agents and tasks defined in Python code. Scaffolded means <code>crewai create crew</code>, then <code>crewai install</code> to set up the venv and dependencies from <code>pyproject.toml</code>, a standard layout, and YAML config files. Scaffolded is the better fit for production because config is separate from code and the structure is consistent across the team. From scratch is quicker for learning and demos.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0015","card":"q-crewai-003-from-scratch-vs-scaffolded","sec":"crewai-s004","q":"What is a good learning path between from-scratch and scaffolded CrewAI projects?","a":"<p>Learn the objects (<code>Agent</code>, <code>Task</code>, <code>Crew</code>) in a single Python file first, so you see exactly what each parameter does. Then move to the scaffolded layout, where the same values live in YAML and decorators wire them up. The concepts are identical, only the packaging differs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0016","card":"q-crewai-004-3-api-keys","sec":"crewai-s005","q":"Compare how OpenAI, Google Generative AI and Anthropic handle API key access and free usage when you set up an LLM for CrewAI. (Version-sensitive: provider policies change.)","a":"<p>At the time of writing: OpenAI requires prepaid credits and shows a key only once. Google AI Studio has a free tier, needs a Google Cloud project and lets you view the key later. Anthropic has a limited free plan and shows a key once. Plans change, so confirm on the provider's site. The practical rule is the same: copy one-time keys immediately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0017","card":"q-crewai-004-3-api-keys","sec":"crewai-s005","q":"Which environment variable names does CrewAI use for the OpenAI, Anthropic and Google providers?","a":"<p><code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code>, <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code> and <code>GOOGLE_API_KEY</code> (Gemini through the Google Generative AI API). These are the standard names that the underlying client libraries read, so no extra wiring is needed once they are in the environment or <code>.env</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0018","card":"q-crewai-004-3-api-keys","sec":"crewai-s005","q":"What is the safe way to supply an API key to a CrewAI project, and what should you never do?","a":"<p>Put it in <code>.env</code> or the shell environment (<code>export OPENAI_API_KEY=...</code>), and keep <code>.env</code> out of version control. Never hard-code a key in <code>Agent(...)</code> or a script, and never commit it. If a key leaks, revoke it and create a new one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0019","card":"q-crewai-004-3-api-keys","sec":"crewai-s005","q":"You lose an OpenAI API key you created earlier. What can you do?","a":"<p>OpenAI shows the secret only at creation, so you cannot view it again. Create a new secret key and revoke the old one. Providers that let you view the key later (such as Google AI Studio) are the exception.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0020","card":"q-crewai-005-4-first-crew-youtube-ideas","sec":"crewai-s006","q":"A CrewAI script fails on the first run with an authentication error and you have not configured an <code>llm</code>. What do you check first?","a":"<p>The default LLM is an OpenAI model (GPT-4 class in the 2024-2025 releases, so version-sensitive), and it needs <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code> in the environment. Export the key, or pass <code>llm=</code> for another provider with that provider's key. Also confirm the key has credits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0021","card":"q-crewai-005-4-first-crew-youtube-ideas","sec":"crewai-s006","q":"What are the four imports needed to build and run a minimal CrewAI crew in one script?","a":"<p><code>from crewai import Agent, Task, Crew, Process</code>. <code>Agent</code> and <code>Task</code> define the workers and their steps, <code>Crew</code> assembles and runs them, and <code>Process</code> selects <code>Process.sequential</code> or <code>Process.hierarchical</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0022","card":"q-crewai-005-4-first-crew-youtube-ideas","sec":"crewai-s006","q":"In CrewAI, how does a <code>{topic}</code> placeholder in a task description get its value, and why is it useful?","a":"<p>It is filled by <code>crew.kickoff(inputs={\"topic\": \"...\"})</code>. The dictionary keys map to placeholders in task descriptions (and agent fields). This makes tasks reusable: the same crew runs on any topic without editing code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0023","card":"q-crewai-005-4-first-crew-youtube-ideas","sec":"crewai-s006","q":"How would you wire a two-agent CrewAI pipeline where the second agent refines the first agent's output?","a":"<p>Define two agents and two tasks, each task assigned with <code>agent=</code>. Put both tasks in <code>Crew(tasks=[first, second], process=Process.sequential)</code> in that order. In sequential mode the first task's output is passed automatically as context to the next one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0024","card":"q-crewai-005-4-first-crew-youtube-ideas","sec":"crewai-s006","q":"In CrewAI, what does <code>verbose=True</code> on a <code>Crew</code> do, and when would you turn it off?","a":"<p>It prints each agent's reasoning, tool calls and results while the crew runs. Use it in development to debug prompts and tool use. Turn it off in production to reduce log noise and avoid logging sensitive intermediate data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0025","card":"q-crewai-006-5-agents","sec":"crewai-s007","q":"What is the design principle for splitting work among CrewAI agents, and what goes wrong if you ignore it?","a":"<p>One agent, one focused responsibility. An agent with a broad role has to juggle several goals in one prompt, so quality drops and its behavior is harder to debug or test. Separate roles (for example researcher versus planner) give clearer prompts and let you give each agent different tools or models.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0026","card":"q-crewai-006-5-agents","sec":"crewai-s007","q":"In CrewAI, can different agents in one crew use different LLMs, and why would you do that?","a":"<p>Yes, each <code>Agent</code> accepts its own <code>llm</code>. You can give a cheap, fast model to simple steps and a stronger model to reasoning or writing steps. This controls cost and latency without lowering quality where it matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0027","card":"q-crewai-006-5-agents","sec":"crewai-s007","q":"What capabilities does a CrewAI agent have beyond answering with an LLM?","a":"<p>It makes decisions guided by its role and goal, uses tools, can delegate to or collaborate with other agents, and can use memory of past interactions. The tools and delegation are what separate an agent from a plain chatbot.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0028","card":"q-crewai-006-5-agents","sec":"crewai-s007","q":"In CrewAI, how is an agent different from a task, and where is each one's behavior specified?","a":"<p>An agent is the worker (who): its <code>role</code>, <code>goal</code>, <code>backstory</code>, tools and LLM define its identity and capabilities. A task is the unit of work (what): its <code>description</code> and <code>expected_output</code> define the job. A task is assigned to an agent with <code>agent=</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0029","card":"q-crewai-007-three-required-attributes","sec":"crewai-s008","q":"In a CrewAI <code>Agent</code>, what are <code>role</code>, <code>goal</code> and <code>backstory</code>, and what does each contribute to the prompt?","a":"<p><code>role</code> states function and expertise (\"Senior Researcher\"). <code>goal</code> is the individual objective that steers decisions. <code>backstory</code> gives persona, experience and extra context. All three are inserted into the agent's system prompt, so they shape tone, focus and how it decides. They are the only required <code>Agent</code> parameters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0030","card":"q-crewai-007-three-required-attributes","sec":"crewai-s008","q":"Why does a precise <code>goal</code> matter more than a clever <code>backstory</code> in a CrewAI agent?","a":"<p>The goal tells the agent what outcome to optimize and when it is done. A vague goal lets it wander or over-produce. The backstory adds style and domain flavor, but it cannot replace a clear objective.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0031","card":"q-crewai-007-three-required-attributes","sec":"crewai-s008","q":"What does this CrewAI <code>Agent</code> raise, and why?","a":"<p>It fails validation because <code>backstory</code> is required. CrewAI's <code>Agent</code> needs <code>role</code>, <code>goal</code> and <code>backstory</code> (Pydantic validation reports the missing field). Add a <code>backstory</code> string.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from crewai import Agent\n\na = Agent(role=&quot;Researcher&quot;, goal=&quot;Find facts&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"crewai-f0032","card":"q-crewai-007-three-required-attributes","sec":"crewai-s008","q":"Which of <code>role</code>, <code>goal</code> and <code>backstory</code> would you edit first if an agent's answers are too generic, and why?","a":"<p>Start with <code>role</code> and <code>goal</code>: make the role specific (domain, seniority) and the goal measurable (what to produce and for whom). Then add concrete context to <code>backstory</code>. Prompt-level specificity is cheaper and more reliable than switching models.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0033","card":"q-crewai-008-two-ways-to-define-agents","sec":"crewai-s009","q":"In CrewAI, what are the two ways to define agents, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>Direct code: <code>Agent(role=..., goal=..., backstory=...)</code> in a <code>.py</code> file. YAML config: definitions in <code>config/agents.yaml</code>, loaded in <code>crew.py</code> with <code>Agent(config=...)</code> and the <code>@agent</code> decorator. Direct code is simpler to learn. YAML separates prompt text from code, which is cleaner with many agents and lets non-developers edit prompts. Use YAML in scaffolded production projects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0034","card":"q-crewai-008-two-ways-to-define-agents","sec":"crewai-s009","q":"What is the trade-off of defining CrewAI agents directly in Python instead of YAML?","a":"<p>It has fewer moving parts and is easy to read in one file, but long prompt text is mixed with logic, and changing wording means editing code. With many agents the file becomes hard to scan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0035","card":"q-crewai-008-two-ways-to-define-agents","sec":"crewai-s009","q":"In a CrewAI project that uses <code>agents.yaml</code>, where should the agent's name be written and what sits under it?","a":"<p>The agent name is the top-level key, and <code>role</code>, <code>goal</code> and <code>backstory</code> are keys beneath it. A skeleton:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>my_agent:\n  role: &gt;\n    Senior researcher\n  goal: &gt;\n    Find accurate facts\n  backstory: &gt;\n    Ten years of experience</code></pre></div>\n<p>The <code>&gt;</code> folded-style scalar lets a long text span lines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0036","card":"q-crewai-009-two-ways-to-define-agents","sec":"crewai-s009","q":"In a CrewAI <code>crew.py</code>, what do <code>@agent</code> and <code>config=self.agents_config[\"name\"]</code> do together?","a":"<p><code>@agent</code> (from <code>crewai.project</code>) marks a method as an agent factory so the crew class can collect it. <code>config=</code> passes the YAML entry for that key (role, goal, backstory) into <code>Agent</code>. A skeleton:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from crewai import Agent\nfrom crewai.project import agent\n\nclass MyCrew:\n    agents_config = &quot;config/agents.yaml&quot;\n\n    @agent\n    def my_agent(self) -&gt; Agent:\n        return Agent(config=self.agents_config[&quot;my_agent&quot;])</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0037","card":"q-crewai-009-two-ways-to-define-agents","sec":"crewai-s009","q":"In a scaffolded CrewAI project, what is the naming rule between <code>agents.yaml</code> keys and <code>@agent</code> methods, and what happens if it is broken?","a":"<p>The method name must match the YAML key. If they differ, the config lookup fails (a key error) or the agent is not wired as intended. Task references such as <code>agent: my_agent</code> in <code>tasks.yaml</code> must also match the key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0038","card":"q-crewai-009-two-ways-to-define-agents","sec":"crewai-s009","q":"In CrewAI, why can you pass extra arguments such as <code>tools=[...]</code> next to <code>config=</code> in an <code>@agent</code> method?","a":"<p><code>config</code> only supplies the text fields that YAML holds. Things that are objects or runtime choices (tool instances, <code>allow_delegation</code>, <code>verbose</code>) cannot be expressed cleanly in YAML, so you pass them as keyword arguments in the Python method, for example <code>Agent(config=self.agents_config[\"name\"], tools=[SerperDevTool()])</code> gives a tool to just that agent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0039","card":"q-crewai-009-two-ways-to-define-agents","sec":"crewai-s009","q":"Why is <code>agents_config = \"config/agents.yaml\"</code> set as a class attribute in a CrewAI crew class?","a":"<p>The <code>@CrewBase</code>-style class machinery (in the <code>crewai.project</code> helpers) loads the YAML files named by <code>agents_config</code> and <code>tasks_config</code> and exposes them as dictionaries on <code>self</code>. Without those attributes, <code>self.agents_config[...]</code> would not exist. In the scaffold the class is decorated with <code>@CrewBase</code>, which does this loading.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0040","card":"q-crewai-010-agent-parameter-groups","sec":"crewai-s010","q":"In CrewAI, what do <code>max_iter</code>, <code>max_execution_time</code>, <code>max_rpm</code> and <code>max_retry_limit</code> control on an <code>Agent</code>?","a":"<p><code>max_iter</code> caps LLM reasoning iterations so an agent cannot loop forever. <code>max_execution_time</code> is a timeout in seconds so it cannot hang. <code>max_rpm</code> limits requests per minute to avoid provider rate-limit errors. <code>max_retry_limit</code> sets how many times to retry after an error, which heals transient failures. Defaults vary by release, so check the docs for the version you use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0041","card":"q-crewai-010-agent-parameter-groups","sec":"crewai-s010","q":"A CrewAI agent keeps looping on a tool call and burns tokens. What settings and design fixes apply?","a":"<p>Lower <code>max_iter</code> so it stops and returns its best answer, set <code>max_execution_time</code>, and use <code>verbose=True</code> to see the loop. Then fix the cause: a clearer <code>expected_output</code>, a tool that returns usable errors, or a narrower tool set. Limits stop the damage but do not fix a bad prompt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0042","card":"q-crewai-010-agent-parameter-groups","sec":"crewai-s010","q":"You hit provider \"rate limit exceeded\" errors when a CrewAI crew runs. Which <code>Agent</code> parameters help?","a":"<p>Set <code>max_rpm</code> to stay under the provider's requests-per-minute limit, and <code>max_retry_limit</code> to retry after failures. You can also reduce parallel (async) tasks, shorten prompts and use a model with a higher quota.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0043","card":"q-crewai-010-agent-parameter-groups","sec":"crewai-s010","q":"In CrewAI, what does <code>respect_context_window=True</code> do, and why is it useful?","a":"<p>It keeps prompts and responses within the LLM's context window. When the conversation grows too large, CrewAI summarizes instead of failing with a context-length error. The trade-off is that summarization can lose detail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0044","card":"q-crewai-010-agent-parameter-groups","sec":"crewai-s010","q":"In CrewAI, what do <code>allow_code_execution</code> and <code>code_execution_mode</code> do, and which mode is safe for production?","a":"<p><code>allow_code_execution=True</code> lets the agent write and run code (default <code>False</code>). <code>code_execution_mode=\"safe\"</code> runs the code in a Docker container, which is the production choice. <code>\"unsafe\"</code> runs directly on the host and is only for trusted environments, since model-written code could harm the machine. Safe mode needs Docker. (Version-sensitive: this option has been reworked in later CrewAI releases, so check current docs.)</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0045","card":"q-crewai-010-agent-parameter-groups","sec":"crewai-s010","q":"In CrewAI, what is <code>function_calling_llm</code> on an <code>Agent</code> and why would you set it?","a":"<p>It is a separate LLM used only for tool calling, while the main <code>llm</code> does the reasoning. A cheaper or more reliable function-calling model lowers cost, or fixes tool-argument errors from a model that is weak at structured calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0046","card":"q-crewai-010-agent-parameter-groups","sec":"crewai-s010","q":"In CrewAI, what do <code>system_template</code>, <code>prompt_template</code> and <code>response_template</code> change, and when would you use them?","a":"<p>They override the structure of the system instructions, the request prompt and the response format sent to the model. Use them when a model or provider needs a specific chat format. For ordinary work, <code>role</code>, <code>goal</code> and <code>backstory</code> are enough and safer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0047","card":"q-crewai-010-agent-parameter-groups","sec":"crewai-s010","q":"In CrewAI, what does <code>allow_delegation</code> do on an <code>Agent</code>, and what is a downside of leaving it on?","a":"<p>It lets the agent hand work to other agents in the crew. A downside is extra LLM calls and unpredictable hand-offs, which increase cost and make runs harder to debug. Set it to <code>False</code> when each agent should do its own task.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0048","card":"q-crewai-010-agent-parameter-groups","sec":"crewai-s010","q":"What is the <code>cache</code> parameter on a CrewAI <code>Agent</code>, and why might you disable it?","a":"<p>It caches tool usage so identical tool calls reuse results (default <code>True</code>). Disable it when tool results must always be fresh, such as live prices or news.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0049","card":"q-crewai-010-agent-parameter-groups","sec":"crewai-s010","q":"What is <code>llm</code> on a CrewAI <code>Agent</code>, and how do you point it at a non-default provider?","a":"<p>It selects the model. Pass a model string such as <code>\"gemini/gemini-2.0-flash\"</code> or an <code>LLM</code> object, and set that provider's API key in the environment. CrewAI routes through LiteLLM-style provider prefixes in the model string (version-sensitive: this holds for the 0.x and early 1.x releases).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0050","card":"q-crewai-011-6-tools","sec":"crewai-s011","q":"Why are tools what make a CrewAI agent \"agentic\"?","a":"<p>Without tools an agent can only produce text from the model's training data. Tools let it act: search the web, read files, query databases, call APIs. The agent decides when to call them and uses the results in later reasoning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0051","card":"q-crewai-011-6-tools","sec":"crewai-s011","q":"Where can CrewAI tools come from, and when would you write your own?","a":"<p>Built-in tools from <code>crewai_tools</code> (such as <code>SerperDevTool</code>, <code>FileReadTool</code>, <code>GitHubSearchTool</code>), LangChain tools, bridge tools like <code>ComposioTool</code>, or a custom class extending <code>BaseTool</code>. Write a custom tool when you need your own API, database or business logic that no existing tool covers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0052","card":"q-crewai-011-6-tools","sec":"crewai-s011","q":"In CrewAI, which package do you import <code>SerperDevTool</code> from, and what extra setup does it need?","a":"<p>From <code>crewai_tools</code> (installed with <code>pip install 'crewai[tools]'</code>), not from <code>crewai</code>. It also needs <code>SERPER_API_KEY</code> in the environment, from serper.dev, because it calls a Google Search API.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0053","card":"q-crewai-011-6-tools","sec":"crewai-s011","q":"How would you check that a CrewAI tool works before giving it to an agent?","a":"<p>Call it directly, for example <code>SerperDevTool().run(search_query=\"AI agents\")</code>, and inspect the output. This separates tool problems (missing key, wrong arguments) from agent or prompt problems.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0054","card":"q-crewai-011-6-tools","sec":"crewai-s011","q":"In CrewAI, how do you give an agent more than one tool?","a":"<p><code>tools</code> takes a list: <code>Agent(..., tools=[search_tool, file_tool])</code>. The agent picks among them by their names and descriptions, so each tool needs a clear description.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0055","card":"q-crewai-011-6-tools","sec":"crewai-s011","q":"In CrewAI, what must a custom tool that extends <code>BaseTool</code> define?","a":"<p>A <code>name</code> and a <code>description</code> (the agent reads these to decide when to use the tool) and a <code>_run</code> method with the logic. A skeleton:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from crewai.tools import BaseTool\n\nclass WordCountTool(BaseTool):\n    name: str = &quot;Word counter&quot;\n    description: str = &quot;Counts words in a text&quot;\n\n    def _run(self, text: str) -&gt; str:\n        return str(len(text.split()))</code></pre></div>\n<p>Import path is version-sensitive: <code>crewai.tools</code> for recent releases, <code>crewai_tools</code> in older ones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0056","card":"q-crewai-011-6-tools","sec":"crewai-s011","q":"Why does the <code>description</code> of a CrewAI tool matter so much?","a":"<p>The LLM chooses tools based on name and description alone. A vague description means the tool is called at the wrong time or never. State what it does, its input and what it returns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0057","card":"q-crewai-011-6-tools","sec":"crewai-s011","q":"Are all CrewAI tools free to use?","a":"<p>No. The library is free, but many tools call paid services (image generation, Composio, search APIs beyond a free tier). Check pricing before giving an agent a tool it can call repeatedly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0058","card":"q-crewai-011-6-tools","sec":"crewai-s011","q":"What does <code>SerperDevTool</code> return, and how does the agent use it?","a":"<p>It returns JSON from Google Search results (titles, links, snippets). The agent reads that text as an observation in its reasoning loop and summarizes or follows it. You do not have to parse it yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0059","card":"q-crewai-012-7-memory","sec":"crewai-s012","q":"Why does CrewAI memory go beyond the LLM's context window, and what does it add?","a":"<p>Context holds only the current prompt. Memory stores information in a retrievable store so agents can recall earlier interactions, learn from past runs and keep coherence across tasks. It uses embeddings and retrieval rather than putting everything into the prompt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0060","card":"q-crewai-012-7-memory","sec":"crewai-s012","q":"In CrewAI (0.x memory system), what are short-term, long-term, entity and contextual memory?","a":"<p>Short-term: recent interactions, kept for the current run using retrieval. Long-term: lessons and patterns persisted across runs. Entity: people, places and organizations and their relationships. Contextual: the combination of the others that keeps a sequence of tasks coherent. External memory is a separate option via providers such as Mem0. (Version-sensitive: newer CrewAI releases replace these types with a unified memory API.)</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0061","card":"q-crewai-012-7-memory","sec":"crewai-s012","q":"How do you enable memory in CrewAI, what is its default, and how do you change the embedding provider it uses? (Version-sensitive: stated for the 0.x releases.)","a":"<p>Memory is off by default. Set <code>memory=True</code> on the <code>Agent</code> or on the <code>Crew</code> for all agents. Embeddings default to OpenAI's in 0.x releases, so a missing OpenAI key breaks memory even if your LLM is another provider. Pass an <code>embedder</code> dictionary naming the provider and model to change it, for example <code>embedder={\"provider\": \"google\", \"config\": {\"model\": \"models/text-embedding-004\"}}</code>. Key names change between releases, so check the docs for yours.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0062","card":"q-crewai-012-7-memory","sec":"crewai-s012","q":"When would you not enable CrewAI memory?","a":"<p>For short, stateless runs where nothing needs to carry over, and where cost or latency matters. Memory adds embedding calls and storage. It can also carry stale or sensitive data into later runs, which is a privacy concern.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0063","card":"q-crewai-012-7-memory","sec":"crewai-s012","q":"What is the difference between passing prior task output through <code>context</code> and CrewAI memory?","a":"<p><code>context</code> passes specific task outputs to specific tasks within one run, explicitly and deterministically. Memory is a retrieval store shared across tasks and potentially across runs, and what gets recalled depends on similarity search. Use <code>context</code> for known dependencies and memory for learned or accumulating information.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0064","card":"q-crewai-013-8-tasks","sec":"crewai-s013","q":"What is a task in CrewAI, and how does it relate to an agent?","a":"<p>A task is the basic unit of work, one piece of the crew's overall goal, with a description, expected output and optional tools and context. Usually one task is performed by one agent, and <code>agent=</code> binds them. One agent may own several tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0065","card":"q-crewai-013-8-tasks","sec":"crewai-s013","q":"Why is task breakdown central to a CrewAI crew's success?","a":"<p>Each task gives an agent a single clear objective and a defined output to hand on. Oversized tasks produce unfocused outputs and errors that spread to later steps, so smaller well-scoped tasks are easier to test and fix.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0066","card":"q-crewai-014-execution-styles","sec":"crewai-s014","q":"How do sequential and hierarchical execution differ in CrewAI, and when would you choose each?","a":"<p>Sequential: tasks run in list order and each output feeds the next. It suits linear pipelines. Hierarchical: a manager agent plans, assigns tasks to agents and reviews results, allowing dynamic or multi-path workflows. Choose hierarchical when the steps cannot be fixed in advance. The cost is more LLM calls and less predictability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0067","card":"q-crewai-015-core-parameters","sec":"crewai-s015","q":"In a CrewAI <code>Task</code>, what is <code>expected_output</code> for, and why include a sample in it?","a":"<p>It defines the shape and content of the result the agent must produce. Adding a short sample acts as few-shot prompting: the model copies the format. This makes outputs consistent and easier for the next task to consume.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0068","card":"q-crewai-015-core-parameters","sec":"crewai-s015","q":"In CrewAI, what are the three core <code>Task</code> parameters, and which are required?","a":"<p><code>description</code> (what to do), <code>expected_output</code> (what the result looks like) and <code>agent</code> (who does it). <code>description</code> and <code>expected_output</code> are required. In a hierarchical crew, <code>agent</code> can be left out because the manager assigns work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0069","card":"q-crewai-015-core-parameters","sec":"crewai-s015","q":"In CrewAI, what does <code>human_input=True</code> on a task do?","a":"<p>It pauses after the agent's answer so a human can review and give feedback before the task is considered done. Use it for human-in-the-loop checks on important results. It makes runs interactive, so avoid it in unattended jobs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0070","card":"q-crewai-015-core-parameters","sec":"crewai-s015","q":"What does the optional <code>name</code> parameter on a CrewAI <code>Task</code> give you?","a":"<p>A readable label for the task, which appears in logs and output objects and helps you identify tasks when you debug or inspect <code>tasks_output</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0071","card":"q-crewai-016-direct-code-travel-crew","sec":"crewai-s016","q":"Where should the instruction \"use a web search tool\" be placed for a CrewAI task, and what must also be true for it to work?","a":"<p>In the task <code>description</code>, but the agent (or the task) must actually have a search tool in <code>tools</code>. Mentioning a tool in the text does not create it. If no tool is attached, the agent can only guess or hallucinate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0072","card":"q-crewai-017-yaml-method","sec":"crewai-s017","q":"In CrewAI, how do you define a task in <code>tasks.yaml</code> and bind it to an agent?","a":"<p>The task name is the top-level key, with <code>description</code>, <code>expected_output</code> and <code>agent</code> beneath it, where <code>agent</code> is the key of an agent in <code>agents.yaml</code>. A skeleton:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>my_task:\n  description: &gt;\n    Research {topic}\n  expected_output: &gt;\n    A bullet list\n  agent: my_agent</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0073","card":"q-crewai-017-yaml-method","sec":"crewai-s017","q":"What are the two name-matching rules when using CrewAI YAML config for tasks?","a":"<p>The <code>@task</code> method name in <code>crew.py</code> must match its key in <code>tasks.yaml</code>, and the <code>agent:</code> value in <code>tasks.yaml</code> must match a key in <code>agents.yaml</code>. A mismatch leads to key errors or tasks that cannot find their agent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0074","card":"q-crewai-017-yaml-method","sec":"crewai-s017","q":"In CrewAI <code>crew.py</code>, how do you load a task from YAML?","a":"<p>Define a method decorated with <code>@task</code> (from <code>crewai.project</code>) that returns <code>Task(config=self.tasks_config[\"name\"])</code>. A skeleton:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from crewai import Task\nfrom crewai.project import task\n\nclass MyCrew:\n    tasks_config = &quot;config/tasks.yaml&quot;\n\n    @task\n    def my_task(self) -&gt; Task:\n        return Task(config=self.tasks_config[&quot;my_task&quot;])</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0075","card":"q-crewai-017-yaml-method","sec":"crewai-s017","q":"In CrewAI YAML, what does <code>&gt;</code> after <code>description:</code> do?","a":"<p>It is YAML's folded block scalar: the indented lines that follow are joined into one string with line breaks folded into spaces. It lets you write long prompts readably without quotes or escaping.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0076","card":"q-crewai-018-managing-task-output","sec":"crewai-s018","q":"In CrewAI, what are <code>output_pydantic</code>, <code>output_json</code> and <code>output_file</code> on a <code>Task</code>, and which would you pick for each use case?","a":"<p><code>output_pydantic</code> takes a Pydantic model class and returns a model instance, for structured data used inside Python. <code>output_json</code> takes a model class and returns a dict, for passing to external APIs. <code>output_file</code> writes the result to a path, for documents. The first two use the same model and differ only in return type.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0077","card":"q-crewai-018-managing-task-output","sec":"crewai-s018","q":"How do you read a CrewAI task result after <code>kickoff()</code>, and which fields are available when?","a":"<p><code>task.output</code> is a <code>TaskOutput</code>. <code>.raw</code> is always set. <code>.pydantic</code> is set only if <code>output_pydantic</code> was used, and <code>.json_dict</code> only if <code>output_json</code> was used. Reading the wrong one gives <code>None</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0078","card":"q-crewai-018-managing-task-output","sec":"crewai-s018","q":"A CrewAI task uses <code>output_pydantic=Model</code> but <code>task.output.json_dict</code> is <code>None</code>. Why?","a":"<p><code>json_dict</code> is filled only when <code>output_json</code> is set. Use <code>.pydantic</code> with <code>output_pydantic</code>, or switch the parameter. <code>.raw</code> is always available.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0079","card":"q-crewai-018-managing-task-output","sec":"crewai-s018","q":"In CrewAI, what does a task return by default, and what happens to it when another task depends on it?","a":"<p>A raw string. It is passed to dependent tasks as text context automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0080","card":"q-crewai-018-managing-task-output","sec":"crewai-s018","q":"Why use a Pydantic model for CrewAI task output instead of asking for JSON in the prompt?","a":"<p>The model defines and validates the schema, so you get typed fields and a clear failure if the shape is wrong, while a prompt-only request can return malformed or inconsistent JSON. The trade-off is that a complex schema can make the model struggle, so keep it small.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0081","card":"q-crewai-018-managing-task-output","sec":"crewai-s018","q":"What does this code do, and what type is <code>plan</code>?","a":"<p>The agent's answer is parsed into the <code>Plan</code> model, so <code>plan</code> is a <code>Plan</code> instance and <code>plan.morning</code> is a string. If the answer cannot be parsed into that shape, the task fails or <code>pydantic</code> is empty. <code>planner</code> is an <code>Agent</code> defined elsewhere.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from crewai import Task\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\nclass Plan(BaseModel):\n    morning: str\n    evening: str\n\nt = Task(\n    description=&quot;Plan a day in {city}&quot;,\n    expected_output=&quot;A structured plan&quot;,\n    agent=planner,\n    output_pydantic=Plan,\n)\n# after crew.kickoff(inputs={&quot;city&quot;: &quot;Rome&quot;})\nplan = t.output.pydantic</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"crewai-f0082","card":"q-crewai-018-managing-task-output","sec":"crewai-s018","q":"In CrewAI, how does <code>output_file</code> on a <code>Task</code> work, and where can you set it in a scaffolded project?","a":"<p><code>output_file</code> takes a relative or absolute path and writes the task result there, for example <code>output_file=\"article.md\"</code> on a final writing or formatting task. In a scaffolded project you can set it either in <code>tasks.yaml</code> as <code>output_file: article.md</code> or in Python as <code>Task(config=..., output_file=\"article.md\")</code>. Only values that must be Python objects, such as tool instances or Pydantic classes, have to be set in <code>crew.py</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0083","card":"q-crewai-019-task-level-tools","sec":"crewai-s019","q":"In CrewAI, what happens when both the agent and the task have <code>tools</code>?","a":"<p>The task's tools override the agent's for that task. If only the agent has tools it uses them, and if only the task has tools those are used. Overriding narrows the choices for that step and reduces non-deterministic tool selection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0084","card":"q-crewai-019-task-level-tools","sec":"crewai-s019","q":"Why attach a tool to a CrewAI task instead of to the agent?","a":"<p>One agent may own several tasks that need different tools. A task-level tool means each task sees only what it needs, such as a search tool on a research task and a database tool on a summary task. It also limits what the agent can do in each step, which is safer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0085","card":"q-crewai-019-task-level-tools","sec":"crewai-s019","q":"In CrewAI, how do you give only one task a search tool?","a":"<p>Pass it in the task: <code>Task(description=..., expected_output=..., agent=a, tools=[SerperDevTool()])</code>, with <code>SerperDevTool</code> imported from <code>crewai_tools</code> and <code>SERPER_API_KEY</code> set.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0086","card":"q-crewai-020-context-dependencies-between-tasks","sec":"crewai-s020","q":"In a sequential CrewAI process, what output does a task receive by default, and how do you change that?","a":"<p>Only the output of the immediately preceding task. Set <code>context=[task_a, task_b]</code> to receive outputs from several tasks, or from a non-adjacent one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0087","card":"q-crewai-020-context-dependencies-between-tasks","sec":"crewai-s020","q":"How would you make a CrewAI summarizing task use the outputs of two earlier tasks?","a":"<p>Put both in its <code>context</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>summary = Task(\n    description=&quot;Compare the findings&quot;,\n    expected_output=&quot;A comparison report&quot;,\n    agent=analyst,\n    context=[task_one, task_two],\n)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Here <code>analyst</code>, <code>task_one</code> and <code>task_two</code> are an <code>Agent</code> and two <code>Task</code> objects defined earlier. Without <code>context</code>, it would see only the task listed right before it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0088","card":"q-crewai-020-context-dependencies-between-tasks","sec":"crewai-s020","q":"In CrewAI, can a task skip a task listed just before it and depend on an earlier one?","a":"<p>Yes. List only the earlier task in <code>context</code>, for example <code>context=[task_b]</code>. The dependency is explicit, so the intermediate task's output is not passed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0089","card":"q-crewai-020-context-dependencies-between-tasks","sec":"crewai-s020","q":"In CrewAI YAML, how do you declare <code>context</code> for a task, and why must the referenced tasks exist?","a":"<p>As a list of task keys under <code>context:</code> in <code>tasks.yaml</code>. The names must match other tasks' keys so CrewAI can resolve them to task objects. A typo gives a lookup error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0090","card":"q-crewai-021-async-execution","sec":"crewai-s021","q":"What does <code>async_execution=True</code> do on a CrewAI <code>Task</code>, and when is it a good fit?","a":"<p>It runs the task in the background without blocking the next tasks. Good for long-running work (scraping, bulk summarizing) and non-critical side jobs (logging, notifications, email). It is a poor fit when the next step needs the result right away, unless that step lists it in <code>context</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0091","card":"q-crewai-021-async-execution","sec":"crewai-s021","q":"In CrewAI, what happens to a downstream task that lists an async task in <code>context</code>?","a":"<p>It waits for the async task to complete before running. An async task that nothing depends on runs in the background and blocks nothing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0092","card":"q-crewai-021-async-execution","sec":"crewai-s021","q":"What are the risks of making many CrewAI tasks async?","a":"<p>Parallel LLM calls can hit provider rate limits and raise cost spikes at once, and ordering is less predictable, so debugging is harder. Use <code>max_rpm</code> and keep async for tasks that are truly independent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0093","card":"q-crewai-021-async-execution","sec":"crewai-s021","q":"In CrewAI, why is \"send a notification email\" a better async candidate than \"write the report\"?","a":"<p>Nothing downstream needs the email's result, so it need not block the pipeline. The report feeds later steps and must finish first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0094","card":"q-crewai-022-9-crews-and-process","sec":"crewai-s022","q":"What does a CrewAI <code>Crew</code> do, and what parameters does it take at minimum?","a":"<p>It is the orchestrator: it holds agents and tasks, decides order via the process, runs execution with <code>kickoff()</code> and collects the final result. Minimum: <code>agents</code> and <code>tasks</code>. Common extras: <code>process</code>, <code>verbose</code>, <code>memory</code>, <code>manager_llm</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0095","card":"q-crewai-022-9-crews-and-process","sec":"crewai-s022","q":"In a sequential CrewAI crew, how is execution order decided?","a":"<p>By the order of the <code>tasks</code> list. Each task runs after the previous one, and output passes forward automatically. Agent order does not matter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0096","card":"q-crewai-022-9-crews-and-process","sec":"crewai-s022","q":"What is wrong with this CrewAI hierarchical crew definition?","a":"<p>It has no <code>manager_llm</code>. A hierarchical process needs an LLM for the manager (or a custom manager agent), for example <code>manager_llm=\"gpt-4o\"</code>. Without it, CrewAI raises a validation error.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>crew = Crew(\n    agents=[a1, a2],\n    tasks=[t1, t2],\n    process=Process.hierarchical,\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"crewai-f0097","card":"q-crewai-022-9-crews-and-process","sec":"crewai-s022","q":"In a CrewAI hierarchical crew, what are the cost and reliability concerns?","a":"<p>The manager LLM makes extra calls to plan, delegate and validate. It can mis-assign work or loop on delegation, so results vary between runs. Test it well and cap iterations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0098","card":"q-crewai-023-10-running-crews-and-reading-output","sec":"crewai-s023","q":"In CrewAI, what does <code>kickoff(inputs={...})</code> return, and which attributes does it expose?","a":"<p>A <code>CrewOutput</code>. <code>.raw</code> is the final output as a string. <code>.pydantic</code> and <code>.json_dict</code> are set if the final task uses <code>output_pydantic</code> or <code>output_json</code>. <code>.tasks_output</code> is a list of <code>TaskOutput</code>, one per task. <code>.token_usage</code> is a token breakdown.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0099","card":"q-crewai-023-10-running-crews-and-reading-output","sec":"crewai-s023","q":"How would you see what each task in a CrewAI crew produced, not only the final result?","a":"<p>Loop over <code>crew_output.tasks_output</code> and print each item's <code>.raw</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for i, task_output in enumerate(crew_output.tasks_output):\n    print(f&quot;Task {i + 1}: {task_output.raw}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0100","card":"q-crewai-023-10-running-crews-and-reading-output","sec":"crewai-s023","q":"Why would you read <code>token_usage</code> from a CrewAI run?","a":"<p>To monitor cost and find expensive steps: it reports total, prompt, completion and cached tokens plus successful requests. Use it to trim prompts, set cheaper models for easy tasks and spot loops.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0101","card":"q-crewai-023-10-running-crews-and-reading-output","sec":"crewai-s023","q":"In CrewAI with <code>verbose=True</code>, what loop do you see in the logs?","a":"<p>The ReAct pattern: thought (reasoning about what to do), action (tool call with input) and observation (the tool result), repeated until the agent gives a final answer. It helps you see why an agent chose a tool or got stuck.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0102","card":"q-crewai-023-10-running-crews-and-reading-output","sec":"crewai-s023","q":"A CrewAI run prints nothing useful and the final output is empty or wrong. What do you check?","a":"<p>Turn on <code>verbose=True</code> and read the thought-action-observation steps. Check the provider key, that the input keys match every <code>{placeholder}</code>, that tools are attached and working, and that <code>expected_output</code> is clear. Then look at <code>tasks_output</code> to find which task went wrong.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0103","card":"q-crewai-023-10-running-crews-and-reading-output","sec":"crewai-s023","q":"In CrewAI, what happens if <code>inputs</code> is missing a key used as a <code>{placeholder}</code> in a task description?","a":"<p>The placeholder cannot be filled, so the run fails with an error or the literal placeholder text reaches the model. Keep the <code>inputs</code> keys in sync with the placeholders.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0104","card":"q-crewai-024-11-project-technical-writing-crew","sec":"crewai-s024","q":"How should you approach designing a multi-agent system in CrewAI: agents first or problem first?","a":"<p>Problem first. Break the problem into steps, turn each step into a task, then decide which agents are needed. Starting from agents tends to give roles without clear work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0105","card":"q-crewai-024-11-project-technical-writing-crew","sec":"crewai-s024","q":"In CrewAI, can one agent own multiple tasks, and when is that sensible?","a":"<p>Yes. For related steps that need the same skills and tools, one agent can do both, for example editing and then formatting. It avoids extra agents and keeps the number of roles small.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0106","card":"q-crewai-025-plan","sec":"crewai-s025","q":"Why would you attach a web-search tool only to the research agent in a content-writing CrewAI crew?","a":"<p>Only that step needs fresh external data. Giving tools to every agent invites needless calls, cost and off-task behavior. Writers and editors work from the text they receive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0107","card":"q-crewai-025-plan","sec":"crewai-s025","q":"Why might you avoid a code-execution tool in a CrewAI writing crew?","a":"<p>In safe mode it requires Docker and it suits only code-centric topics (such as Python). It adds setup and risk without value for general articles.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0108","card":"q-crewai-025-plan","sec":"crewai-s025","q":"Which <code>context</code> dependencies would you set in a research, outline, write, edit, format pipeline in CrewAI?","a":"<p>Research has none. Outline depends on research. Write depends on research and outline, since it needs both the facts and the structure. Edit depends on the draft. Format depends on the edited text. Explicit <code>context</code> lets a later task use a non-adjacent earlier one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0109","card":"q-crewai-026-agents-yaml","sec":"crewai-s026","q":"How do you keep CrewAI agent prompts maintainable as the number of agents grows?","a":"<p>Keep role, goal and backstory in <code>agents.yaml</code> and use folded scalars (<code>&gt;</code>) for long text. Prompt edits then do not touch code, diffs are readable and reviewers can focus on wording.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0110","card":"q-crewai-026-agents-yaml","sec":"crewai-s026","q":"In CrewAI agent YAML, why write the <code>goal</code> in terms of the outcome rather than the activity?","a":"<p>The goal guides decisions. \"Produce an accurate outline that covers the research\" lets the agent judge when it is done, while \"do outlining\" does not. Outcome-based goals give more consistent output.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0111","card":"q-crewai-027-tasks-yaml","sec":"crewai-s027","q":"In CrewAI task YAML, what belongs in the file and what belongs in <code>crew.py</code>?","a":"<p>Plain data (<code>description</code>, <code>expected_output</code>, <code>agent</code>, <code>context</code>, and a file path such as <code>output_file</code>) can live in YAML. Values that must be Python objects, such as tool instances or Pydantic output classes, are passed in the <code>@task</code> method in <code>crew.py</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0112","card":"q-crewai-027-tasks-yaml","sec":"crewai-s027","q":"Why does a CrewAI <code>context</code> list in YAML make dependencies easier to review?","a":"<p>The data flow is visible in one place: each task lists the tasks it reads from. A reviewer can check the graph without reading Python.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0113","card":"q-crewai-027-tasks-yaml","sec":"crewai-s027","q":"In CrewAI task YAML, why include a Markdown instruction in <code>expected_output</code> for a writing task?","a":"<p>The agent follows the expected format, so asking for Markdown, headings and code blocks gives a file that needs little reformatting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0114","card":"q-crewai-028-crew-py-complete","sec":"crewai-s028","q":"In CrewAI, why is <code>from crewai.project import agent, task, crew</code> needed in <code>crew.py</code>?","a":"<p>These decorators register methods: <code>@agent</code> and <code>@task</code> mark factories, and <code>@crew</code> marks the method that assembles the crew. The framework uses the markers to collect the objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0115","card":"q-crewai-029-crew-py-complete","sec":"crewai-s028","q":"In a CrewAI crew class using decorators, what do <code>self.agents</code> and <code>self.tasks</code> contain inside the <code>@crew</code> method?","a":"<p>Lists of every object built by the <code>@agent</code> and <code>@task</code> methods, collected automatically in definition order. This is why you do not list them by hand. Task order in the class sets sequential execution order.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0116","card":"q-crewai-029-crew-py-complete","sec":"crewai-s028","q":"What do you check in a CrewAI project if the tasks run in the wrong order under <code>Process.sequential</code> with the <code>@crew</code> decorator?","a":"<p>The order the <code>@task</code> methods are defined in the class, since <code>self.tasks</code> follows it. Reorder the methods or use <code>context</code> for explicit dependencies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0117","card":"q-crewai-029-crew-py-complete","sec":"crewai-s028","q":"Complete this CrewAI <code>@crew</code> method skeleton. What does each argument do?","a":"<p><code>agents</code> and <code>tasks</code> use the auto-collected lists from the decorated methods. <code>process</code> sets the run order to sequential. <code>verbose=True</code> prints run logs. The <code>@crew</code> decorator marks it as the method that builds the crew so <code>MyCrew().crew()</code> returns a ready <code>Crew</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from crewai import Crew, Process\nfrom crewai.project import crew\n\nclass MyCrew:\n    @crew\n    def crew(self) -&gt; Crew:\n        return Crew(\n            agents=self.agents,\n            tasks=self.tasks,\n            process=Process.sequential,\n            verbose=True,\n        )</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"crewai-f0118","card":"q-crewai-030-main-py-and-running","sec":"crewai-s029","q":"In a scaffolded CrewAI project, what are the four CLI commands and what do they do?","a":"<p><code>crewai run</code> runs the workflow. <code>crewai train</code> trains with human feedback to improve outputs. <code>crewai replay</code> restarts from a specific task. <code>crewai test</code> runs the crew a number of times and reports performance. Each calls the matching function in <code>main.py</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0119","card":"q-crewai-030-main-py-and-running","sec":"crewai-s029","q":"Why use <code>crewai replay</code> instead of re-running a whole CrewAI crew after a late-stage failure?","a":"<p>Replay restarts from a chosen task using stored earlier outputs, so you do not pay again for early expensive steps such as research. It is the way to iterate on the later part of a pipeline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0120","card":"q-crewai-030-main-py-and-running","sec":"crewai-s029","q":"In a scaffolded CrewAI <code>main.py</code>, how do you start the crew with inputs?","a":"<p>Build an inputs dictionary and call <code>kickoff</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def run():\n    inputs = {&quot;topic&quot;: &quot;GraphQL basics&quot;}\n    try:\n        MyCrew().crew().kickoff(inputs=inputs)\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>MyCrew()</code> creates the class, <code>.crew()</code> returns the <code>Crew</code>, <code>.kickoff(inputs=)</code> starts it. <code>MyCrew</code> stands for your crew class.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"crewai-f0121","card":"q-crewai-030-main-py-and-running","sec":"crewai-s029","q":"Why is a <code>try/except</code> in <code>run()</code> of a CrewAI <code>main.py</code> a mixed blessing?","a":"<p>It prints a friendly error, but it can hide stack traces and still exit with success status. In production, log the exception and re-raise, or exit non-zero so automation detects failure.</p>","hand":true}];
